      "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"All three blind responses identify the ship primarily as the Church — the vessel of salvation carrying believers safely through the world's storms toward eternal life — grounded in Noah's ark typology (Gen 6–9; 1 Pet 3:20–21), the Gospel storm-calming and boat narratives, and Peter's barque (Luke 5). Secondary/alternative readings include the individual life-voyage of faith, worldly commerce and pride (ships of Tarshish, Tyre in Ezek 27, Babylon in Rev 18), and Jonah's flight.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book defines the ship as an organized city, state, or governed body riding upon the 'sea of peoples': rulers as pilots, inhabitants and servants as crew, armies as defenders, wealth as cargo. It adds that the ship may be broken while its people survive. Citations are exclusively Tyre (Ezek 27; Isa 23), Babylon's maritime trade (Rev 18), Paul's shipwreck (Acts 27), and Jonah's ship — none of the Gospel boat or ark texts.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Ship = the Church, the vessel of salvation preserving believers through the sea of the world.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Ship = an organized polity (city, state, or governed body) sailing on the sea of peoples, whose structure may perish while its people survive.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book drops the entire ecclesial case-set (Gospel boats, Noah's ark, Peter's barque) and builds solely on texts the consensus files under secondary meanings (Ezek 27, Isa 23, Rev 18, Acts 27, Jonah 1). It also reclassifies those texts: where the consensus reads Tyre/Babylon ships as emblems of commerce, wealth, and pride destined for judgment, the book reads the ship itself as the governed polity, with wealth demoted to cargo within it. Acts 27 and Jonah, treated by the consensus as life-voyage or flight-from-God allegories, become illustrations of a state's structure breaking while its population survives.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The consensus's central claim is that the ship IS the vessel of salvation (the Church): staying aboard is safety. The book's ship is a worldly political structure whose destruction is compatible with — even distinct from — the survival of its people ('the ship may be broken while its people survive,' per Acts 27:44). Accepting the book's single identification requires rejecting Church-as-ship as the symbol's meaning; the salvific-vessel referent is replaced by a perishable polity riding the sea of peoples.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to cores, the consensus says ship = Church/vessel of salvation; the book says ship = organized city-state/governed body. These are different central referents, not a boundary adjustment of the same referent. The book is not NOVEL because Tyre-as-ship (Ezek 27) appears in the consensus alternatives, but the book does not merely elevate that alternative — it reframes it from 'commerce/pride' to 'polity as corporate vessel' and makes it the sole meaning, while its 'broken ship, surviving people' principle inverts the consensus logic in which the ship itself is the means of preservation. That is replacement of the central identification, hence DIVERGENT, with partial reclassification of the shared texts.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"One could argue REFINED via BOOK_BROADENS: the Church is itself a 'governed body,' so the book's category could subsume the ecclesial reading rather than replace it, and Ezekiel 27 — where a city is explicitly and extensively portrayed as a ship with pilots, crew, army, and cargo — is arguably the Bible's only explicit ship allegory, making the book's choice of primary referent textually defensible rather than contradictory.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
